),
updated_existing_event AS (
    UPDATE events
    SET
        title = 'UNT Physics Colloquium Presents Dr. Bing Lv',
        description = 'Title: Quality Matters: How High-Quality Crystals Reveal New Quantum Phenomena – Three Examples 

 

Abstract: Single-crystalline materials provide the foundation for uncovering intrinsic electronic, magnetic, and optical properties, and are essential for the technologies that drive modern society. Despite decades of progress in bulk and epitaxial growth techniques, the availability of materials in sufficiently pure, stoichiometric, and highly crystalline form remains a central bottleneck in experimental condensed matter physics. In many cases, the discovery of new physical phenomena is limited not only by theoretical understanding, but also by the ability to synthesize materials of in sufficiently pure, stoichiometric, and highly crystalline form.

In this talk, I will first introduce our group’s capabilities in crystal and thin-film growth at the University of Texas at Dallas, with an emphasis on strategies to access challenging and metastable materials systems. I will then highlight three representative examples where advances in synthesis have enabled new directions in quantum materials research. These include quasi-one-dimensional topological materials such as Bi₄X₄(X=I, and Br), which provide a platform for exploring novel  topological states; two-dimensional magnetic materials such as CrSBr, where exceptional crystal quality reveals rich spin and excitonic phenomena; and ultrahigh thermal conductivity materials such as boron arsenide (BAs), which enable fundamental studies of phonon transport and have potential for next-generation thermal management.  Through these examples, I hope to convince you that by pushing crystal growth beyond conventional limits, we can create new materials platforms where intrinsic properties emerge clearly, opening pathways to uncover and control novel electronic, magnetic, and thermal phenomena.',
        location_id = (SELECT location_id FROM resolved_location),
        start_date_time = TIMESTAMP '2026-03-31 15:30:00',
        end_date_time = TIMESTAMP '2026-03-31 16:30:00',
