        'Presented by the School of Mathematics & Science Foundations in the UNT College of Science, the Best Practices in STEM Teaching Seminar Series invites leading experts in STEM education to share their knowledge with the UNT community throughout the spring semester. Faculty who teach introductory-level courses are encouraged to attend and learn about evidence-based strategies to improve teaching and learning.

Dr. Stephanie M. Gardner is a physiology educator and discipline-based education researcher at Purdue University. She has a doctorate in Physiology from UW-Madison and did a postdoctoral fellowship in Neuroscience at Johns Hopkins University. Her research includes revealing student reasoning and approaches with science practices like constructing mechanistic explanations, experimentation, and analyzing and visualizing biological data. Newer work centers on examining how interdisciplinary research, collaboration, and identity are developed within a Biology Integration Institute. Dr. Gardner leads graduate student and faculty professional development programs around studentcentered instruction, mentoring, and incorporating research into undergraduate classrooms. She is the new Director of Mentorship Success in the Department of Biological Sciences where she works with undergraduate and graduate students as well as faculty to support the development and maintenance of strong, positive mentorship practices. She is a PULSE Fellow (Partnership for Undergraduate Life Sciences Education), a Senior Editor at CBE - Life Sciences Education, and serves on the editorial review boards of Advances in Physiology Education and Frontiers in Education-STEM Education.

On April 10, Dr. Gardener will be presenting her talk, "GraphSmarts: Backward design for biological skills - From a student model to student data to instructor-designed teaching tools for graphing biological data," in the UNT Chemistry Building, room 109.',
